import {lesson as L, sources as S} from './format.mjs';
export const intelligence = [
L('math-1','Vectors and linear algebra','vector',S.linear,
'Compute a dot product and interpret it as alignment or a weighted sum.',
'A vector can describe a position, velocity or features of a piece of data. The meaning comes from the coordinate system. The same operation can compare directions in geometry or combine features in a model.',
['An n-dimensional vector is an ordered list of n numbers. Addition and scalar multiplication operate coordinate by coordinate. The Euclidean norm is the square root of the sum of squared components.',
 'For vectors a and b, a·b = Σᵢ aᵢbᵢ. It equals |a||b|cos(θ) for nonzero vectors. Dividing by their norms gives cosine similarity, which compares direction independently of length.',
 'A matrix maps an input vector to an output vector. Each row’s dot product with the input gives one component of that output. Matrix composition represents applying one linear map after another.',
 'Projection onto a unit vector u gives scalar coordinate x·u. To recover the vector projection, multiply that scalar by u. A CPU or a GPU may use vectorized instructions to calculate many products together.'],
'Use a=(3,4) and b=(1,0). The dot product is 3, a has norm 5, b has norm 1, and cosine similarity is 0.6. In vector search, doubling a gives the same cosine but doubles raw dot product.',
'A dot product depends on magnitudes and alignment. Only the normalized form isolates direction.',
'Vectors carry coordinates. Dot products weight and sum. Matrices pack many linear transformations into one operation.',
['What is the cosine similarity between (1,0) and (0,4)?',['0','1','4'],0,'The vectors are perpendicular. Their dot product and cosine similarity are both zero.','Multiply corresponding components and add.']),
L('graphics-1','Pixels and rasterization','raster',S.graphics,
'Use triangle edge tests to decide whether a pixel sample is covered.',
'A monitor shows samples on a grid. A scene might describe triangles and materials in continuous coordinates. Rasterization decides which samples a triangle covers after projection.',
['A vertex stage transforms world coordinates to clip space. Clipping removes portions outside the view volume. Perspective division and a viewport transform then place positions on the screen.',
 'For a directed edge A→B and sample point P, compute cross(B−A, P−A) in two dimensions. Its sign indicates which side of the edge contains P. With consistent triangle orientation, three matching signs indicate coverage.',
 'Barycentric coordinates express a point as weighted contributions from triangle vertices. Interpolating attributes requires perspective correction when they were defined before projection.',
 'A depth test compares the new fragment’s depth with the stored depth and resolves visibility. Production rasterizers specify edge ownership between adjacent triangles and may test multiple samples per pixel.'],
'The teaching triangle uses screen points (1,1), (7,1) and (2,7). Move the sample point and inspect the edge signs. Compare a sample such as (2.5,2.5) with (8.5,2.5). The lab treats points exactly on an edge as covered.',
'Pixel coverage and visibility are separate. A triangle can cover a sample and still lose a depth test.',
'Transform geometry to the screen, test coverage, interpolate attributes, then evaluate depth and shading.',
['What is evidence that a sample is outside a nondegenerate triangle?',['One edge sign differs from the others','Its depth is positive','Its pixel coordinates are not whole numbers'],0,'A point inside the triangle lies consistently on the interior side of all three edges.','Compare the point to each edge independently.']),
L('gpu-1','SIMT, warps and divergence','warp',S.cuda,
'Compute active lanes under a branch and explain why divergence costs instruction slots.',
'A GPU can issue one instruction across a group of lanes, each with its own data. If different lanes need different instructions at a branch, the group can execute the paths with different lanes active.',
['In CUDA, threads are grouped into blocks. On NVIDIA GPUs a warp contains 32 threads. SIMT presents a thread abstraction to programmers while the scheduler issues instructions for warps.',
 'When lanes in the same warp take different paths, active masks identify which lanes participate in each instruction. Paths can execute with other lanes inactive. If entire warps take different branches, there is no within-warp divergence from that decision.',
 'Coalescing depends on the memory addresses requested by active lanes. Nearby, suitably aligned requests can use fewer memory transactions. Occupancy measures resident warps relative to the limit and can help hide latency, but registers and shared memory constrain it.',
 'A GPU is often fast when work is uniform and independent. Divergence, random access or synchronization can reduce effective throughput. Modern execution architectures have additional scheduling details beyond the lab’s equal-cost path model.'],
'If 16 lanes take each side of a branch of equal cost, two issued paths yield 32 useful lane-operations out of 64 lane slots in this model. Compare with 32 lanes taking one path.',
'A CUDA block can contain multiple warps. Shared memory and block synchronization have different semantics from a lane mask.',
'Group threads into warps. Count active lanes per issued path. Optimize memory access as well as branch uniformity.',
['Which arrangement avoids within-warp divergence for a two-path branch?',['Odd lanes take A, even lanes take B','Every lane in a warp takes the same path','Exactly one lane takes B'],1,'A uniform warp needs to execute only its chosen path for that decision.','Think at warp granularity.']),
L('ml-1','Regression and gradient descent','gradient',S.ml,
'Train a one-parameter regression and predict how learning rate affects the loss.',
'A model is a rule with adjustable parameters. Training measures how wrong the rule is on examples and changes the parameters in a direction that reduces that error.',
['Fit ŷ = wx on a small dataset. With squared loss L = Σ(ŷ−y)²/(2n), the derivative with respect to w is Σ((wx−y)x)/n.',
 'Gradient descent updates w ← w − η ∂L/∂w. A positive gradient means increasing w locally increases the loss, so the update decreases it. Learning rate η controls the size of the move.',
 'Too small a rate moves slowly. Too large a rate can overshoot and even diverge. For this one-dimensional quadratic, the loss curvature is the mean of x² and stable convergence requires 0 < η < 2/curvature.',
 'Validation measures on separate examples help choose model settings. A held-out test set estimates generalization once those choices are fixed. Training loss alone measures fit to observed training examples.'],
'For data (1,2), (2,4), (3,6), the optimum is w=2. At w=0 the gradient is −28/3. With rate 0.1 the first update is about 0.9333. Step the lab and then try rate 0.6.',
'A gradient gives a local direction and scale. An arbitrarily large move along a descending direction can still land at higher loss.',
'Define the objective, derive its gradient, tune a stable step size and measure generalization.',
['If the derivative is negative and η is positive, what happens in one gradient descent update?',['w decreases','w increases','w must become zero'],1,'Subtracting a negative derivative gives a positive update.','Track the two minus signs.']),
L('dl-1','Neurons and backpropagation','backprop',S.grad,
'Use the chain rule to trace how a scalar loss produces gradients for two weights.',
'A neural network is a composition of functions. Backpropagation asks how changing each input to that composition would affect the final error.',
['Consider z=w₁x, h=tanh(z), ŷ=w₂h and L=(ŷ−y)²/2. The forward pass computes each intermediate value in order.',
 'Starting with ∂L/∂ŷ = ŷ−y, pass derivatives backward. The gradient for w₂ is (ŷ−y)h. The gradient for w₁ is (ŷ−y)w₂(1−h²)x.',
 'Autodiff systems record operations and save some intermediate values that backward computation needs. If multiple downstream paths depend on a value, their gradient contributions add.',
 'An optimizer uses gradients to update the parameters. Backpropagation computes gradients. The parameter update is a separate operation. Saturating nonlinearities can make gradients small along some paths.'],
'Use the lab’s x=1, target=1, w₁=w₂=0.5. Check that both gradients are negative and a small positive-rate update increases both weights on the first step.',
'A deeper network is not trained by manually deriving a long closed expression for every parameter. The chain rule can be applied locally on the computation graph.',
'Forward computes values. Backward multiplies local derivatives and adds contributions. The optimizer applies updates.',
['If a node feeds two later operations, how do their gradient contributions combine?',['Add','Always multiply','Keep only the larger'],0,'Different paths contribute additively to the total derivative of the loss with respect to that value.','Think of the change in total loss caused by moving one shared value.']),
L('ai-1','Tokenization and embeddings','token',S.tokens,
'Explain how text becomes integer IDs and how the model looks up vectors for those IDs.',
'An LLM starts with a sequence of integers. A tokenizer chooses the integers, and a learned embedding table gives each integer a vector the network can work with.',
['A vocabulary maps byte or text sequences to token IDs. Byte-pair encoding starts from a small symbol inventory and learns useful pair merges from data. Inference applies those merge rules in the learned priority order.',
 'A word may split into several tokens, and a token may include whitespace. Unicode representation and normalization depend on the tokenizer’s design. Model and tokenizer must agree on IDs and vocabulary.',
 'For vocabulary size V and hidden width d, the embedding table has V rows of d values. Indexing row i retrieves token i’s embedding. Context-dependent representations emerge in later layers.',
 'The lab uses invented vectors and a tiny ordered merge list. Real models learn both vocabulary statistics and embedding parameters on much larger datasets.'],
'Enter banana and inspect how the toy tokenizer merges b+a and n+a. Change punctuation to see how an unknown symbol gets a fallback ID in this lab. Practical byte-based tokenizers can represent arbitrary byte sequences.',
'The ID is an index, so nearby integer IDs need not mean similar things. Similarity depends on learned vectors and context.',
'Tokenization segments text and gives IDs. Embedding lookup converts IDs to vectors. Layers add context.',
['What determines the embedding vector of a token at input lookup?',['Its ID selects a row in the embedding table','Its ID is multiplied by text length','The current context always changes the table row'],0,'The model gathers vectors by ID. Later layers update representations using context.','Imagine a matrix with one row per vocabulary item.']),
L('ai-2','Transformer attention','attention',S.attention,
'Calculate attention weights from query-key scores and explain causal masking.',
'A token’s current representation can read information from other positions. It asks a learned question with a query vector, scores what each position offers using keys, and combines the values it retrieves.',
['Learned linear projections turn the input state into queries, keys and values. A head computes query–key dot products, then scales by √dₖ to control the score magnitude as key dimension grows.',
 'Add a mask before softmax. In causal attention a query can read its own position and earlier ones. Giving future positions a score of −∞ makes their normalized weights zero.',
 'For each query, softmax exponentiates the permitted scores and divides by their sum. The output for that query is the weighted sum of value vectors.',
 'Multiple heads use separate projections. The transformer block combines their results and includes other operations, such as a feed-forward block and residual paths. The lab isolates one head with tiny fixed keys and values.'],
'Select position 0 and turn causal masking on. The first weight is 1 because only the first key is visible. Then move to position 2 and change the query vector to see the distribution shift.',
'An attention weight tells you how that head mixes value vectors at this operation. It does not by itself explain the model’s final output.',
'Project to Q, K and V, score and mask the keys, normalize over allowed positions and combine the values.',
['With causal attention, which keys can a query at position 1 use in a sequence indexed from zero?',['Only position 1','Positions 0 and 1','All future and past positions'],1,'A causal mask removes keys with index larger than the query’s index.','The current token is permitted.']),
L('inference-2','Autoregressive inference and KV caches','kv',S.kv,
'Calculate KV cache size and explain why cached keys and values survive across decode steps.',
'Generating a token requires looking at the prefix already produced. A cache stores the attention keys and values for that prefix so a model can avoid projecting all previous tokens again.',
['Prefill processes the initial prompt and fills a cache for each attention layer. At each decode step, generate the current token’s queries, keys and values, attend over cached entries and append new keys and values.',
 'Earlier positions in a causal transformer do not depend on future tokens, so appending one token does not alter keys and values already computed for earlier positions. Their memory remains necessary for unrestricted attention.',
 'The dense cache size is approximately 2 × layers × KV heads × head dimension × sequence length × bytes per element × batch. The factor of two counts keys and values.',
 'Grouped-query attention uses fewer KV heads than query heads, reducing cache size. Sliding-window models may keep only recent positions in some layers. Serving engines can page cache allocations to reduce wasted space.'],
'With 32 layers, 8 KV heads, dimension 128, 2048 tokens and two-byte elements, a single sequence uses 256 MiB in this model. Double sequence length and observe that it doubles the cache.',
'KV caching saves work on repeated older projections. New queries still need to read relevant historical keys and values.',
'Prefill creates cache entries. Decode appends entries. Budget memory based on actual KV heads and context.',
['If KV heads halve and other settings stay fixed, what happens to cache size?',['Doubles','Halves','Remains the same'],1,'Each KV head has its own key and value entries. The formula is linear in KV heads.','Count KV heads instead of query heads.']),
L('inference-3','Quantization and model compression','quant',S.quant,
'Quantize weights using integer levels and measure reconstruction error.',
'A weight stored with fewer bits takes less memory, but the nearest available represented value may differ from the original. Quantization chooses how to assign those limited levels.',
['For symmetric quantization, choose a positive scale s and integer range −Q…Q, where Q=2^(b−1)−1. Map a weight w to clip(round(w/s), −Q, Q). Reconstruct as ŝ = sq.',
 'Choosing s as max absolute weight divided by Q avoids clipping the calibration set. A single large outlier can raise the scale and increase rounding errors for many small weights.',
 'Per-channel or group scales represent different local ranges. Asymmetric methods use a zero point for shifted ranges. Accuracy depends on where and how the model uses quantized values.',
 'Quantization reduces representation width. Speed depends on hardware, memory bandwidth, kernel support and overhead. Pruning removes structure or values. Distillation trains another model to imitate target behavior.'],
'Compare a five-weight group with its 4-bit reconstruction, then switch to 8 bits. Watch how the mean squared error changes if you increase the calibration maximum while keeping the original group fixed.',
'Lower bit width does not give an equal speedup across devices. A kernel may need to unpack or dequantize the weights.',
'Choose the range and scale, round and clip, reconstruct and measure the error.',
['What can happen if one outlier sets an unusually large scale?',['Small values round to fewer distinct levels','Every value becomes exact','The bit width automatically increases'],0,'A larger scale spaces levels further apart, which can make small differences disappear.','Compare weights with the distance between representable levels.']),
L('frontiers-1','Chiplets and advanced fabrication','yield',S.chip,
'Compare monolithic and chiplet integration and calculate a simplified defect-limited die yield.',
'A large design can be made as one die or split into smaller dies integrated in a package. Splitting changes fabrication risk, interconnect cost and which process is appropriate for each function.',
['Lithography patterns layers, transistors implement electronic switching, and a stack of metal interconnect carries signals and power. More advanced processes can improve density or efficiency, within physical and manufacturing constraints.',
 'A simple Poisson yield model assumes independent uniformly distributed defects and gives Y=e^(−DA), where D is defects per unit area and A is die area. It illustrates why large dies can be more likely to intersect a defect.',
 'Chiplet designs can test dies separately and assemble known-good dies, then use advanced packaging and die-to-die interfaces to connect them. Designers often mix dense compute dies with I/O made on another process.',
 'Smaller dies can reduce material lost per defect. Packaging adds assembly yield losses, link latency, bandwidth constraints and integration cost. The lab isolates die-level yield, so it cannot predict packaged unit cost.'],
'Compare a 4 cm² die with 1 cm² tiles at 0.2 defects/cm². Individual yields are about 45% and 82%. Without screening or reuse, requiring four randomly chosen tiles to all work brings the probability back to roughly 45%.',
'Individual tile yield is different from final package yield. The die-level curve alone does not settle manufacturing cost.',
'Yield depends on area and defect density. Known-good dies change how defects waste material. Packages introduce new tradeoffs.',
['Under Y=e^(−DA), what happens to a single die’s yield when area goes up at fixed positive defect density?',['It increases','It stays fixed','It falls'],2,'Higher area makes the exponent more negative, reducing the probability of no defect.','Treat D as a positive constant.'])
];
